WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
This animation is zooming out by a factor of 10 every 2 seconds.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.003
Maybe you've seen things like this before, conveying the mind-boggling scale

00:00:08.003 --> 00:00:11.989
of our universe, but here, in this video, you and I are going to continue the

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:16.026
saga through the many moments of delightful ingenuity throughout Human history

00:00:16.026 --> 00:00:19.960
that led us to first discover how far away objects in the cosmos really are.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.636
Appreciating how we know these distances is to

00:00:22.636 --> 00:00:24.920
me more amazing than the distances themselves.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.207
This is part 2 of a collaboration with Terrence Tao,

00:00:28.207 --> 00:00:30.460
and it's okay if you haven't yet seen part 1.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.966
Each video should be relatively self-contained, but for context,

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:36.877
we left off with Kepler's ingenious method for deducing the

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:39.740
shapes of all of the orbits of the planets around the Sun.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.670
So people knew what the Solar System looked like,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.290
but they still didn't have an exact sense of scale,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:49.776
and this left astronomers hungry to measure any distance that they could in this system,

00:00:49.776 --> 00:00:53.405
like maybe how far away a given planet is from Earth at a given moment,

00:00:53.405 --> 00:00:56.580
since that would be enough to lock everything else into place.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.565
Now, I'll admit, while I had vaguely learned about how this was done before,

00:01:00.565 --> 00:01:03.920
I definitely had not appreciated the cleverness of the details.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.400
You could measure distances to a planet like Venus,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.989
take two measurements on different sides of the Earth,

00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:13.330
around the time of Captain Cook, when they were travelling to discover

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.460
Australia and so forth.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.400
Part of the reason for this was the scientific mission.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.280
I mean, they wanted to know the distance to Venus and Mars and so forth.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.546
They wanted people to take precise measurements,

00:01:21.546 --> 00:01:24.635
say one in Greenwich in the UK and one somewhere in the southern hemisphere,

00:01:24.635 --> 00:01:26.440
at exactly the same time of the same object.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.862
The key idea here is that as you sail down to the southern hemisphere

00:01:30.862 --> 00:01:34.445
and you observe a given object up in the sky, its position in the sky,

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:36.817
say relative to the background constellations,

00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:40.147
will appear to shift up as the angle of your line of sight slowly

00:01:40.147 --> 00:01:41.560
changes with your position.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.100
We call this parallax.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.440
It is the same parallax that we use with binocular vision.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.308
Our eyes are a certain distance apart, and so we can determine depth for

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:51.040
any distance that's not too much larger than the distance between our eyes.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.920
So now we figured out how to make two eyeballs on different sides of the Earth.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.009
Now if you want to turn this into a measurement,

00:01:57.009 --> 00:01:59.463
you first need to understand this line right here,

00:01:59.463 --> 00:02:03.600
connecting the two different observation points, both its distance and its direction.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.324
Because people knew the size of the Earth, and they could tell

00:02:06.324 --> 00:02:08.479
where they were on the Earth, that would be taken care of.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.797
Then the hard part is for the first observer to take a precise

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:14.360
enough measurement of the viewing angle to this object.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.360
They need to deduce this angle here, and for the second observer to do likewise.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.284
If you can do that, you have a triangle where you know all three of the angles,

00:02:22.284 --> 00:02:24.420
and you also know one of the side lengths.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.026
This means with just a little bit of trigonometry,

00:02:27.026 --> 00:02:30.908
you can figure out any other side length, which for example tells you how far away this

00:02:30.908 --> 00:02:32.540
object is from one of the observers.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.620
Now the way I'm drawing it here, it makes it look almost easy.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.031
But for the real-world measurement, you have to

00:02:38.031 --> 00:02:39.980
keep in mind just how far away everything is.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.430
If we zoom out so that they're both looking at something as far away as the Moon,

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.800
it would look something like this.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.800
But the Moon, cosmologically speaking, is really quite close.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.875
So even if both of them are looking at the nearest planet, Venus,

00:02:51.875 --> 00:02:57.203
when it is at its absolute closest to Earth, it's around 39 million kilometers away,

00:02:57.203 --> 00:03:00.400
which is over 6,000 times the radius of the Earth.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.005
So zooming back in to those two observers, even if they were as far away

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:08.335
as you could get them, one at the top of the Earth and another at the

00:03:08.335 --> 00:03:11.760
bottom of the Earth, these two lines of sight would be almost parallel.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.782
If you work out the little bit of trigonometry,

00:03:14.782 --> 00:03:18.353
if they're both looking at Venus at its absolute closest point,

00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:22.259
the difference in angle between these two lines is about 1 arcminute,

00:03:22.259 --> 00:03:24.100
which is 1 sixtieth of a degree.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.829
So, if this is going to work, your measurements have to be extremely precise,

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:32.265
and you have to be absolutely sure that both observers are really

00:03:32.265 --> 00:03:34.660
looking at the same thing at the same moment.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.940
Now at the time clocks were not good enough that they could actually

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.157
just say at this specific time, take this measurement,

00:03:40.157 --> 00:03:43.260
also you're not guaranteed that you could even find the planet and so forth.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.880
But there are these transits, there's this thing called the transit of Venus.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.875
Sometimes Venus travels along the Sun, and so you can

00:03:49.875 --> 00:03:54.020
just time exactly when Venus hits the edge of the Sun.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.700
This is the part I had not appreciated, in particular how sensitive everything really is.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.101
So, up in the Northern Hemisphere, if you're watching the transit of Venus,

00:04:02.101 --> 00:04:03.580
maybe it looks something like this.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.694
And far away, at some point down in the Southern Hemisphere, due to parallax,

00:04:07.694 --> 00:04:11.868
Venus would appear higher up, and you essentially want to know exactly how much higher

00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:12.060
up.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.019
Now, this animation is greatly exaggerating the difference.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.160
In reality, the two would look much, much more similar, more like this.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.752
And remember, there was no photography, so it's not

00:04:22.752 --> 00:04:25.180
like they could take pictures and closely compare them.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.936
When you look at the Sun up in the sky, it spans around 32 arcminutes of viewing angle,

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.720
and this is something they would have been able to measure.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.028
The deviation in viewing angle to Venus for these

00:04:36.028 --> 00:04:38.880
two observations would be a fraction of an arcminute.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.676
So to measure exactly what that fraction was, each

00:04:41.676 --> 00:04:44.920
observer would not try to directly describe where it was.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.697
Instead, they would measure the duration of the transit,

00:04:47.697 --> 00:04:51.413
how long it takes from the moment that Venus's silhouette first appears on the disk

00:04:51.413 --> 00:04:52.740
to the moment that it leaves.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.590
Critically, they also would have known how fast Venus and the Sun are moving

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:01.795
through the sky at this time, and this would let them calculate how long it should

00:05:01.795 --> 00:05:06.000
take Venus's apparent position to traverse the distance of, say, one Sun diameter.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.960
It turns out to be around seven hours.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:12.051
I won't dwell too much on the details for how to calculate this, but at a high level,

00:05:12.051 --> 00:05:14.842
if you think about Earth and Venus both orbiting the Sun,

00:05:14.842 --> 00:05:18.211
the angle of this line of sight right here, from Earth through Venus,

00:05:18.211 --> 00:05:21.627
is something they would have understood well, since, thanks to Kepler,

00:05:21.627 --> 00:05:25.381
they knew the relative shapes of the orbits and how long it takes each planet

00:05:25.381 --> 00:05:26.440
to go around the Sun.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.566
So in principle, they could calculate how long it should take for this

00:05:30.566 --> 00:05:34.460
line to scan over those 32 arcminutes in the sky that the Sun occupied.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:39.586
The point is that by measuring the durations of the transit from these two different

00:05:39.586 --> 00:05:44.774
locations, that could tell you the lengths of these two lines drawn across the Sun's

00:05:44.774 --> 00:05:45.140
disk.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.055
These are very similar, but measurably different,

00:05:48.055 --> 00:05:51.792
and with a little bit of circle geometry, the lengths of those lines will

00:05:51.792 --> 00:05:55.529
tell you how far apart they are, that fraction of an arcminute separating

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:56.640
the two observations.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.019
And then this, in turn, from everything that we discussed before,

00:06:01.019 --> 00:06:05.540
lets you deduce how far away Venus is in terms of the distance between those observers.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.160
That is just so clever to me.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.288
It was Edmund Halley who originally came up with this idea,

00:06:11.288 --> 00:06:13.940
but sadly he didn't live long enough to see it come to fruition.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:18.100
Before moving on, I simply have to tell you the story of Guillaume Le Gentil.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.473
He was another explorer, also tasked with making

00:06:20.473 --> 00:06:22.740
one of these measurements for the transit of Venus.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.457
Specifically, he was going for the one in 1761,

00:06:25.457 --> 00:06:29.832
but he was delayed by the Seven Years' War, and still at sea when the transit happened,

00:06:29.832 --> 00:06:31.920
so he was unable to make the measurement.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.549
Now the next transit of Venus was going to be eight years later,

00:06:35.549 --> 00:06:38.157
but after that it wouldn't be for another 105 years,

00:06:38.157 --> 00:06:41.799
so he decided to extend his journey, do a bunch of other explorer things,

00:06:41.799 --> 00:06:42.980
and catch the next one.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.738
And by 1769, he was all set up in the Philippines ready to go,

00:06:46.738 --> 00:06:50.200
but on that day it was cloudy, so he couldn't see anything.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.825
Once he finally got back to France, he found that he had been declared dead,

00:06:53.825 --> 00:06:56.900
his wife had remarried, and his relatives had all plundered his estate.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.562
Now remember, the reason people cared so much was that having this one

00:07:00.562 --> 00:07:04.590
measurement was enough to lock in place the scale of the whole solar system,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.880
so once they had it, they would automatically know every other distance involved.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.981
This was important actually because you need to do

00:07:10.981 --> 00:07:12.940
this in order to work out the distance to the Sun.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.940
The distance to the Sun is the most important rung of the ladder.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.540
It's called the astronomical unit.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.240
Almost everything beyond the solar system is measured in terms of the astronomical unit.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.060
And so they really needed really accurate estimates.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.411
Given what we just saw, you might be able to guess how you can use

00:07:27.411 --> 00:07:30.660
this astronomical unit to measure the distance to nearby stars.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.039
But before we get to that, there's actually one more clever deduction that you can make

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:37.434
once you know this distance, a little closer to home,

00:07:37.434 --> 00:07:39.120
and incredibly important for physics.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.540
Romer was measuring Jupiter, and Jupiter has a small moon called Io.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.040
Io orbits Jupiter really, really fast because it's really close to Jupiter.

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:50.160
Our moon takes 28 days to go around the Earth.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:51.740
Io takes 42 hours.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:52.860
Let's whip it.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:54.400
Yeah, it's extremely fast.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:58.480
If you observe Jupiter on a telescope, you see Io, which is this white dot,

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:01.740
go in behind Jupiter's shadow, and then out, in, out, in, out.

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:05.660
Romer was marking down one precise moment for each one of these cycles.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.060
You see, Io goes dark when it falls into Jupiter's shadow,

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:11.920
which it does every cycle because Jupiter is just so big.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.280
As it emerges, it becomes bright again.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.135
Watching it through a telescope night after night,

00:08:17.135 --> 00:08:19.820
you can mark the exact time that it pops back into view.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.765
And if you go out with a telescope and you do this a whole bunch,

00:08:22.765 --> 00:08:25.620
what you'll notice is that these happen in 42-hour increments.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.000
Like clockwork, except that it wasn't clockwork.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:32.221
So, Romer was observing Io go back and forth, back and forth for months,

00:08:32.221 --> 00:08:36.392
and he observed that at different times of the year, Io was ahead of schedule,

00:08:36.392 --> 00:08:37.659
Io was behind schedule.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.299
So, I'll give you a picture.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:42.100
Earth goes on the Sun, Jupiter also goes on the Sun.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:49.828
So, when you're measuring the orbit of Io, it was 20 minutes

00:08:49.828 --> 00:08:54.800
earlier when the Earth was on the same side as Jupiter.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.480
Then, when the Earth was on the opposite side of Jupiter, it took 20 minutes longer.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.800
There was a delay in the orbit.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.360
So, he measured this after some very precise calculations.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.981
And Huygens realized that the reason for this is because

00:09:07.981 --> 00:09:12.100
light was taking 20 minutes to traverse this extra distance.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:17.200
So, two astronomical units is traversed in about 20 minutes.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:17.920
That's so clever!

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.080
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.740
Historically, this was all before the measurement to Venus.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.700
So, at that time, they didn't actually know the true astronomical unit.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.174
So, their estimates for the speed of light, frankly,

00:09:28.174 --> 00:09:29.980
don't look that impressive if you see them.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:34.060
But, at that time, it was not even obvious that light had a speed.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.100
For most experiments you could do down on Earth at that time, it just looks instantaneous.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.080
But at astronomical scales, it's actually really slow.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:44.680
And this very clever way to measure its speed by observing Io,

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:49.216
imprecise though it was, laid the groundwork for more precise experiments down on Earth,

00:09:49.216 --> 00:09:52.580
once people had at least a rough sense of how fast it really was.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.480
I also really like this because it comes back around to the distance ladder.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.975
These days, with technology, the more accurate way that we measure the

00:09:59.975 --> 00:10:03.520
distances to planets like Venus is not with parallax, but using radar.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.440
But this, of course, relies on knowing the speed of light.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:11.160
And now, let's finally leave our solar system and measure the nearby stars.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.660
Again, you can use parallax.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:18.017
It's essentially identical reasoning for how we got the distance to Venus,

00:10:18.017 --> 00:10:22.078
only this time, the two observation points are not opposite sides of the Earth,

00:10:22.078 --> 00:10:24.160
they're opposite sides of Earth's orbit.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.724
That is, you measure the star at one time of the year,

00:10:28.724 --> 00:10:32.216
and then you wait six more months for the Earth to be on the other side of the Sun,

00:10:32.216 --> 00:10:34.460
make another measurement, and compare the two angles.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.040
Again, for demonstration purposes, I'll be drawing this star unrealistically close.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:42.799
In principle, over the course of six months, the line of sight

00:10:42.799 --> 00:10:45.254
from the Earth to a star slowly changes angle,

00:10:45.254 --> 00:10:49.380
so the position of that star in the sky should slowly drift over those months.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.760
And it's very subtle, but you can actually see this in practice.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:57.613
This right here is a time lapse of a very zoomed-in view for our nearest neighbor,

00:10:57.613 --> 00:11:00.040
Proxima Centauri, taken over six months.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.046
And you can see that over that time, it does gently

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:05.260
drift with respect to the background stars.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.144
But as before, the mind-blowing part is to appreciate just how subtle the effect is,

00:11:10.144 --> 00:11:13.348
and therefore how impressive it is that people were able to use

00:11:13.348 --> 00:11:15.700
this for calculation back in the 19th century.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.540
Let's actually work out the math here.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.469
Suppose a star is a given distance d away, and to make everything easy,

00:11:21.469 --> 00:11:25.716
let's assume it's perpendicular to this connecting line between our two observation

00:11:25.716 --> 00:11:26.120
points.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:29.798
This angle right here represents half of the change

00:11:29.798 --> 00:11:32.620
in viewing angle between those two observations.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:36.455
The tangent of that angle, opposite over adjacent,

00:11:36.455 --> 00:11:41.127
gives you the ratio between one astronomical unit and the distance between

00:11:41.127 --> 00:11:42.560
that star and our Sun.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.773
Rearranging a little bit, this change in angle looks

00:11:45.773 --> 00:11:48.420
like two times the inverse tangent of that ratio.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:50.300
So, what is that ratio?

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.987
Well, for that closest star that I mentioned, Proxima Centauri,

00:11:54.987 --> 00:11:59.616
it's over 40 trillion kilometers away, which is over four light-years,

00:11:59.616 --> 00:12:02.680
and around 260,000 times an astronomical unit.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:06.670
Now if you plug that in, it means that this change in angle from

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.960
the two observation points is a tiny, tiny portion of a degree.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.380
Let me see if I can show you how small this is.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.860
This right here is a circle representing 360 degrees of full viewing angle.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:20.727
And if we zoom in real close to one degree, you'll remember I mentioned earlier

00:12:20.727 --> 00:12:23.126
that the Sun, and also the Moon for that matter,

00:12:23.126 --> 00:12:25.378
take up about half a degree of viewing angle,

00:12:25.378 --> 00:12:28.560
and we often think of this as being divided into 60 arc-minutes.

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:31.911
Well, if you zoom in even further to one arc-minute,

00:12:31.911 --> 00:12:34.540
this is commonly divided into 60 arc-seconds.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:39.080
At its largest, Jupiter will span about 50 arc-seconds of viewing angle.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:46.480
So, this parallax for Proxima Centauri, the nearest star, is only about 1.5 arc-seconds.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.413
As another way to put that in perspective, this is about the same

00:12:50.413 --> 00:12:53.284
angular size as if you held out a dime in front of you,

00:12:53.284 --> 00:12:57.540
but two and a half kilometers away, a mile and a half down from where your eye is.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.200
And that's the closest star.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.640
It only gets worse from there.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.580
The first measurement of this kind was successfully done in 1838 by Friedrich Bessel.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.080
It wasn't Proxima Centauri.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.480
They wouldn't have been able to know which star was closest.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.954
But over the course of the next century, there was a monumental effort

00:13:12.954 --> 00:13:16.240
for many more measurements to try to catalog as many stars as we could.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.440
But this parallax idea only really works for a tiny portion of our galaxy.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.160
So to figure out the full size of the Milky Way, that requires a new idea.

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:30.800
Roughly speaking, the Milky Way is this...

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:31.860
I can't draw a spiral.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.343
Okay, this is again maybe a place where your graphics

00:13:35.343 --> 00:13:37.560
are going to be much better than what I draw here.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:42.340
Alright, so our sun is on one of the arms.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.741
So by using parallax, by about the 19th century,

00:13:47.741 --> 00:13:52.878
there was about maybe a thousand stars close enough to the sun that they knew

00:13:52.878 --> 00:13:54.920
how far away these stars were.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.000
Maybe about 10, 100 light years away.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.740
So they knew how far away these stars are, and they knew how bright they looked.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:05.560
And so they knew how bright they were by the inverse square law of light propagation.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.466
Just to spell this out a little more explicitly,

00:14:08.466 --> 00:14:12.656
if you imagine you have some star in space emitting a bunch of light in all directions,

00:14:12.656 --> 00:14:16.560
then at a given distance away, all of that light is evenly spread along a sphere.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:20.433
So if you're that distance away and you're measuring the brightness with some

00:14:20.433 --> 00:14:24.909
little patch of area, maybe that represents your camera or a photo sensor or your pupil,

00:14:24.909 --> 00:14:28.581
something like that, the amount of light you're receiving depends on the

00:14:28.581 --> 00:14:31.900
proportion of this total sphere that your little area represents.

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.888
So for example, if you were twice as far away,

00:14:34.888 --> 00:14:38.911
that same beam of light that you had been measuring up close is now spread

00:14:38.911 --> 00:14:40.360
along four times the area.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:45.195
So out at this distance, if you used the same photo sensor with the same area as before,

00:14:45.195 --> 00:14:47.840
it would only be measuring a quarter as much light.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:52.804
In general, the amount of light that you see, the apparent brightness of the star,

00:14:52.804 --> 00:14:57.231
is proportional to its absolute brightness, the total amount of light it's giving away,

00:14:57.231 --> 00:15:00.500
divided by the square of the distance between you and that star.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:04.710
So for all of those stars whose distance we could measure using parallax,

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:08.516
by also measuring the apparent brightness, astronomers could know their absolute

00:15:08.516 --> 00:15:09.080
brightness.

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:13.104
The other thing they could observe is the color of all of these stars,

00:15:13.104 --> 00:15:17.140
the frequency of light that they're emitting, which is actually incredibly helpful.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.180
For example, you can start looking for patterns in this data.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.908
Imagine you organize all of your stars onto a plot,

00:15:22.908 --> 00:15:25.900
where in the x direction, we're going to sort them by the color.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.827
The common kind of backward-seeming convention is for higher-frequency light

00:15:29.827 --> 00:15:33.320
like blue to go on the left and lower-frequency like red to be on the right.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:37.389
Then in the y direction, you can sort according to the absolute brightness of these

00:15:37.389 --> 00:15:40.777
stars, which again, you can deduce by knowing their distance and using the

00:15:40.777 --> 00:15:41.680
inverse-square law.

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:45.180
A thousand points of data of intensity and color.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.440
And this is main sequence.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.580
Most stars live on the sequence.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:54.880
This is known as the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, initially created around 1911.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:58.349
The data that went into it though represents decades of effort,

00:15:58.349 --> 00:16:01.993
a lot of it coming from this group of women at the Harvard College Observatory,

00:16:01.993 --> 00:16:03.860
commonly known as the Harvard computers.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.027
The diagram exposes how the color of a star can

00:16:07.027 --> 00:16:09.460
tell you something about its absolute brightness.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:14.028
This main sequence represents one very common type of star, it includes our Sun,

00:16:14.028 --> 00:16:18.140
during a phase of its evolution when it's burning hydrogen in its core.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:22.877
Stars in this category that are bigger and brighter also tend to burn hotter,

00:16:22.877 --> 00:16:26.160
and they have blackbody radiation more in the blue direction,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:30.660
while those that are smaller burn cooler and they radiate more in the red direction.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:35.456
So, if you see some star very far off, way too far for parallax measurements,

00:16:35.456 --> 00:16:38.933
and if you also know that it should fall into this main sequence,

00:16:38.933 --> 00:16:42.830
more on that in a moment, its color can tell you its absolute brightness,

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:44.200
you fit it to this curve.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.913
And then by using its apparent brightness with the inverse-square law,

00:16:47.913 --> 00:16:49.560
you can deduce how far away it is.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.060
Now you might ask, how would you know that a star should fall in this main sequence?

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:55.935
The full diagram is much more splotchy than a clean line,

00:16:55.935 --> 00:16:59.540
it's got a lot of different components representing different types of stars.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:03.599
The thing to keep in mind is that the color information you get from a

00:17:03.599 --> 00:17:07.220
star is much richer than what a single number on this x-axis could convey.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:11.535
For example, this right here represents all of the relative intensities

00:17:11.535 --> 00:17:15.119
for light that we get from our Sun across many different wavelengths.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:16.900
It's what we call its spectrum.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:20.703
This is the usual pattern you see, you have a general lump representing

00:17:20.703 --> 00:17:23.859
the blackbody radiation, with certain wavelengths greatly reduced.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:28.520
Those correspond to the wavelengths of light absorbed by the atoms in the Sun.

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.385
And where specifically those absorption lines fall

00:17:31.385 --> 00:17:34.020
will tell you what the atoms are in the star.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.620
And from that, it helps classify what kind of star it even is.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:42.441
So for example, while those Harvard computers were doing a bunch of detailed analysis

00:17:42.441 --> 00:17:47.008
of spectra from all these stars, some of them, including Antonia Maury and Annie Cannon,

00:17:47.008 --> 00:17:51.420
came up with very useful classification systems for these stars based on the spectra.

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:55.505
You can then use that classification scheme to tell you which cluster you

00:17:55.505 --> 00:17:59.260
would expect a given star to fall in in this Hertzsprung-Russell diagram.

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:02.416
For example, whether it should be in this main sequence,

00:18:02.416 --> 00:18:04.880
or if it's something else entirely, like a red giant.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:08.530
And then once you have this law, you can then go all throughout the galaxy,

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:09.540
and you can out-measure.

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:14.840
But it stops working past the galaxy because each individual star becomes too faint.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:19.060
The galaxy is only a really tiny portion of the full universe.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.800
Okay, so we have one tiny little galaxy here.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.340
So there's all the other galaxies all over.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.708
Yeah, and so in a galaxy, most stars, you cannot measure the brightness of a single star,

00:18:31.708 --> 00:18:34.660
because there's just billions of them all crammed in the same place.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.248
But there are some amazingly bright stars, they're called Cepheids,

00:18:37.248 --> 00:18:40.160
they're supergiant stars that are thousands of times brighter than the Sun.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:43.600
Some of them live in our own galaxy, and some of them live in other galaxies.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.040
They still blend in, but you can still kind of measure their brightness.

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:48.900
These stars are what are called variable stars.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.360
So most stars, they stay the same intensity for all time.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:56.760
But the Cepheid stars, their brightness oscillates, so they have a period of intensity.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.640
Like every 20 days, every 10 days, they oscillate.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:03.060
And it turns out that the bigger the Cepheid, the brighter it is, the longer the period.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:07.200
Henrietta Swan Leavitt, who was a 20th century astronomer,

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:11.591
she measured all the Cepheids that she could in her own galaxy,

00:19:11.591 --> 00:19:15.022
and she plotted the period against the intensity,

00:19:15.022 --> 00:19:21.060
and she found basically a linear law that the brighter the Cepheid, the longer it took.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:24.280
That, like the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram before, gave a standard candle.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:29.279
That if a galaxy happened to have a Cepheid in it, you could just observe it,

00:19:29.279 --> 00:19:32.060
work out its period, that gave you this intensity.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.815
So now you know how bright it should be, and you know how bright it looks,

00:19:35.815 --> 00:19:36.980
you can measure its distance.

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.817
That gave the next rung of the ladder, so a couple

00:19:39.817 --> 00:19:42.880
thousand galaxies where you can measure the distance.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:45.740
But that again is only a tiny portion of the universe.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:47.900
You ever seen powers of ten?

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.180
Oh yeah, yeah, kind of zoom and zoom and zoom.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:59.440
So now this is a local cluster where, thanks to Levitt, you could work out the distances.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.400
And then there's the rest of the universe.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:04.700
But it's enough to get data of some kind.

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:05.700
Yes, yes.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.341
And so, Hubble, Edwin Hubble, was measuring all these galaxies,

00:20:09.341 --> 00:20:11.320
and they have certain spectral lines.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:14.890
For example, there's a lot of hydrogen around each of these galaxies,

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:18.400
and so there's certain spectral lines of hydrogen that get absorbed.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:21.960
And so there's certain lines that are missing, almost missing in a spectrum.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.807
But he noticed that for some of the galaxies, the spectrum was

00:20:25.807 --> 00:20:29.120
shifted to red from where it should be, but not all of them.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:30.540
And so he measured it.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:36.380
So he measured this redshift against distance, and again he got a linear relationship.

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:40.642
There's actually a famous plot in his paper, which is now called Hubble's law,

00:20:40.642 --> 00:20:42.840
that the redshift is proportional to the distance.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:46.296
Nowadays we know why this is true, because general relativity

00:20:46.296 --> 00:20:49.000
predicts that the universe is expanding at a uniform rate.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:53.121
And the further away things are, the more they recede and the faster they recede,

00:20:53.121 --> 00:20:54.460
the more redshift they have.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.800
But again, this is a predictable law.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:01.120
So if you have a galaxy far, far away, if you know what its redshift is,

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:05.920
it just measures a spectrum, you apply this law, you can work out its distance.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:10.320
So this is the largest rung of the ladder that we know to date.

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:15.065
The entire bubble of the observable universe, which we

00:21:15.065 --> 00:21:19.820
think is about 20% of the whole universe, we don't know.

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:24.764
Pretty much every object in the universe that emits light or some sort of radiation,

00:21:24.764 --> 00:21:28.380
we can now measure the distance, usually by seeing how spectral lines shift.

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.880
And so you can start mapping things, you can do 3D pictures of the universe.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:34.960
There's something called the Sloan Galactic Survey, which is trying to do this.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:39.624
For this visualization, what I did is read in some data from the Sloan Digital Sky

00:21:39.624 --> 00:21:42.900
Survey, which aggregates data about a huge number of galaxies,

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:46.800
including their angle in the sky, and then critically their distance away,

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.100
as measured by redshift.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.549
This set doesn't include all of the angles in the sky,

00:21:50.549 --> 00:21:52.300
that's why it has this kind of wonky shape.

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:55.063
This represents about half a million different galaxies,

00:21:55.063 --> 00:21:56.840
visualized as a simple point cloud.

00:21:57.340 --> 00:22:01.181
And in a sense, what you're looking at is a snapshot of our little corner

00:22:01.181 --> 00:22:05.180
of the universe, measured out to a radius of about 1.6 billion light years.

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:10.070
And one thing that has been discovered is that galaxies are not just randomly

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:14.140
strewn out in space, they form these filaments, they form these massive structures.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:17.400
So a galaxy is itself part of these much larger structures.

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:20.860
The thing is, that's actually predicted by theory, or at least by experiment.

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:24.292
So we can run in a computer billions of virtual galaxies,

00:22:24.292 --> 00:22:26.820
opening by gravity for billions of years.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:32.073
And what we have found with simulations is that the galaxies, as they evolve,

00:22:32.073 --> 00:22:36.980
do organize into strands that do resemble the strands that we're beginning to see.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:41.870
So that is, in some sense, some confirmation that all our theory is basically correct.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:46.060
If you're using redshift, what's the error that it'll give you on the distance?

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:47.400
That's a good question.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.764
It's hard to calibrate at very large scales because

00:22:50.764 --> 00:22:54.260
we have very few other ways to measure the distance.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.420
The gravitational black hole things, those are the most exciting.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.902
Normally to measure distances at this distance,

00:23:00.902 --> 00:23:03.920
you need to stack together a huge amount of rungs of the ladder.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:05.940
First of all, the distance of the Sun, and then from

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:07.700
that parallax to work out distance to nearby stars.

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:11.020
And then from that, you can work out using main sequence fitting

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:13.693
distance to other stars in the galaxy, and then Hubble's law,

00:23:13.693 --> 00:23:16.280
no, there's a Cepheids between that, and then Hubble's law.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:20.180
You can use the gravitational measurements to directly measure the distance.

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:23.128
Unfortunately, I don't quite remember, they're kind of like standard sirens,

00:23:23.128 --> 00:23:24.060
that's what they're called.

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:28.011
There's a certain amount of energy that's released by a black hole collision,

00:23:28.011 --> 00:23:30.760
and so you kind of know how loud they are in absolute terms.

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.980
But you can also measure how loud they are from the observatory.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.510
And they're billions and billions of times fainter at that point because

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:39.820
of the vast distance drop, but they can use that to infer the distance.

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:43.000
That, they cross-checked against the Hubble, the redshift calculations.

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:45.060
It matched within like 10%.

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:45.700
That's reassuring.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:47.560
Yeah, yeah, no, it is very reassuring.

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.080
Although that 10% is controversial.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.200
It seems like there's something a bit wonky with Hubble's law at very large scales.

00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:54.940
Like, the same 10% is showing up elsewhere too.

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:58.660
It's an ongoing mystery exactly what is the cause of this 10% anomaly.

00:23:58.900 --> 00:24:00.280
And is one of our laws of physics wrong?

00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:03.483
There's something very basic called the Copernican principle that is an

00:24:03.483 --> 00:24:06.570
article of faith now since the Copernican revolution that we believe the

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:09.700
laws of the universe are pretty much the same everywhere in the universe.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.720
And this is a fundamental assumption that allows us to extend the latter.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:16.300
And it's always rewarded us in the past.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.440
It always fits at least in the rules of physics.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:19.700
It's ever been self-consistent.

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:24.660
But there's this slight 9% anomaly, 10%, and it's an ongoing area.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:27.040
The subject is a living subject, astronomy.

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:30.980
But we've gotten a long way from that poor graduate student walking down the...

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.600
A long walk down Egypt.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:34.660
Yes.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.857
Maybe this goes without saying, but the cosmic distance

00:24:39.857 --> 00:24:43.040
ladder is too broad a topic for two videos to fully convey.

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:46.060
After showing an early view of this on Patreon, for example,

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:50.540
a number of people expressed sadness that there was no discussion of type 1a supernovas.

